{
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "on-requirements-saved",
  "description": "DLC SuperCharge D-minus: fires when a Kiro Spec requirements.md is saved. Advances state.md through Phase 1 (analyze-requirements) -> 2a (trigger scan) -> 2b (conditional reviews) -> 2c (ready for design). Shell work is consolidated into dlc_bridge.hooks.on_requirements_saved (Python module) to avoid Kiro shell-execution buffer corruption.",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileEdited",
    "patterns": [".kiro/specs/**/requirements.md"]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "DLC SuperCharge - On Requirements Saved (Phase 1 -> 2c chain).\n\nStep 0 (notify the user IMMEDIATELY, BEFORE any Bash or other tool call): Surface this exact line as plain chat text so Kiro renders it visibly in the conversation:\n\n  ⏳ DLC: starting analyze-requirements in the background — typically 4–6 minutes; Phase 2b reviews afterward add another 5–8 minutes if recommended. Don't re-prompt; I will surface results when the bridge completes.\n\nThis is essential: Kiro chat does not stream the long-running Bash subprocess output, so without this pre-flight notice the user sees an idle chat and assumes the task is finished.\n\nStep 1 (init: debounce + slug + state + bridge + id-propagate): Use Bash to run exactly this single command (no chaining):\n\n  uv run python -m dlc_bridge.hooks.on_requirements_saved --source <triggering-file>\n\nReplace <triggering-file> with the path that fired this hook. Capture stdout verbatim. The wrapper prints structured KEY=value lines and ends with one of: PROBE_DEBOUNCED, BRIDGE_FAILED, or HOOK_INIT_DONE.\n\n- If output contains PROBE_DEBOUNCED: another fire is in flight, stop silently.\n- If output contains PROBE_SELF_FIRE (paired with HOOK_INIT_SKIPPED): stop silently — this fire was triggered by our own previous write to requirements.md (id_propagate) and there is no new work to do. See Issue #8.\n- If output contains BRIDGE_FAILED: surface the BRIDGE_EXIT value and the bridge's stderr to the user, then stop.\n- Otherwise parse SLUG and PRD from the output.\n\nStep 2 (read plugin's review recommendations from the PRD): The PRD includes a '## Recommended formal reviews' section produced by the analyze-requirements skill — this is the authoritative source of truth for which Phase 2b reviews to run. Read the PRD at the printed PRD path. Find the recommendations section and extract any review marked 'Recommended' (skip ones marked 'Not recommended', 'Defer', or 'N/A'). Map each marked /dlc:review-<domain> to its short name:\n  - /dlc:review-security    -> security\n  - /dlc:review-ux          -> ux\n  - /dlc:review-a11y        -> a11y\n  - /dlc:review-performance -> performance\n\nBuild a comma-separated list from the marked rows. If the recommendations section is missing OR all rows are marked not-recommended, skip Step 3 entirely. Do NOT re-implement keyword scanning — trust the plugin's decision.\n\nStep 2.5 (notify before reviews dispatch, BEFORE the Step 3 Bash): If <domain-list> is non-empty, surface this exact line as plain chat text:\n\n  ⏳ DLC: dispatching Phase 2b reviews (<domain-list>) in parallel — typically 5–8 minutes. Don't re-prompt.\n\nIf <domain-list> is empty (all reviews 'Not recommended'), skip this notice and skip Step 3.\n\nStep 3 (review dispatch via bridge): Use Bash to run exactly this single command (replacing <triggering-file> and <domain-list>):\n\n  uv run python -m dlc_bridge.hooks.on_requirements_saved --source <triggering-file> --stage reviews --domains <domain-list>\n\nThe wrapper dispatches the heavyweight /dlc:review-{security,ux,a11y,performance} skills in parallel via the bridge, each in autopilot mode with scoped output under .dlc/<slug>/. These are FULL Phase-2b pre-design reviews of the codebase using the source PRD as context, producing reports at:\n  - .dlc/<slug>/analysis_output/SECURITY_REVIEW_REPORT.md\n  - .dlc/<slug>/designs/ux-review.md\n  - .dlc/<slug>/analysis_output/A11Y_REVIEW_REPORT.md\n  - .dlc/<slug>/analysis_output/PERFORMANCE_REVIEW_REPORT.md\n\nExpect HOOK_REVIEWS_DONE (all OK) or HOOK_REVIEWS_PARTIAL (one or more failed). On partial, surface which domains failed but continue to finalize.\n\nStep 4 (finalize: advance state to 2c): Use Bash to run exactly this single command:\n\n  uv run python -m dlc_bridge.hooks.on_requirements_saved --source <triggering-file> --stage finalize\n\nExpect HOOK_FINALIZE_DONE in the output.\n\nStep 5 (surface): Print a 3-line summary: 'Phase 1->2c chain complete for <slug>. PRD: .dlc/<slug>/requirements.prd.md. Reviews dispatched: <domain-list-or-none>.' End with the literal token <HOOK_DONE>."
  }
}
